<p class="lead">The Bear Paw grip is the name many archers give to the classic Bear Archery bow grip: a hand-filling wood handle on Bear&rsquo;s traditional bows that settles your palm in the same spot every shot. Whether you searched &ldquo;bear paw bow grip,&rdquo; &ldquo;bear bow grip&rdquo; or &ldquo;bear archery grips,&rdquo; this guide explains how Bear grips are shaped, how wrist angle and material affect accuracy, and how to tell if a grip actually fits your hand.</p>

<p>One honest note before we start: we could not find a current Bear Archery product officially sold as the &ldquo;Bear Paw grip.&rdquo; So this Better Outdoors guide covers the grip style people mean when they use that name, plus Bear bow grips in general, both traditional and compound. If you are really looking for the German Bearpaw brand, we cover that too.</p>

<?= tool_cta('bow-finder') ?>

<h2 id="h-understanding-the-philosophy-behind-the-bear-paw-grip">What People Mean by the &ldquo;Bear Paw&rdquo; Grip</h2>
<p>Bear Archery built its name on traditional bows, and the grips on those bows share a recognizable feel: a full, rounded handle with a defined throat and a heel that fills the base of your palm. Retailers selling Bear take-down risers often describe a signature &ldquo;heal horn&rdquo; grip, and many shooters casually call that hand-shaped contour a &ldquo;bear paw&rdquo; grip.</p>
<p>The thinking behind it is simple:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Consistent placement.</strong> A shaped throat and heel guide your hand to the same spot every time.</li>
  <li><strong>Pressure through the bones.</strong> The grip encourages you to push the bow with the meat of your thumb, lined up with your forearm, instead of squeezing.</li>
  <li><strong>Hunting practicality.</strong> A full wood grip is comfortable with cold hands or thin gloves and doesn&rsquo;t need extra parts to work.</li>
</ul>
<p>On Bear compound bows, the idea is the same, but the execution is different: a slimmer molded grip or bolt-on side plates on the riser. More on that below.</p>

<h2 id="h-grip-shape-and-wrist-angle">Grip Shape and Wrist Angle</h2>
<p>Every bow grip falls somewhere on a spectrum from low wrist to high wrist. This angle decides where your hand pushes on the bow, and it is the single biggest factor in how a grip feels.</p>
