<p>A smooth wood or slim side-plate surface lets the bow settle into your hand naturally. A very tacky grip can tempt you to grab the bow at the shot, which is one of the fastest ways to torque arrows left or right.</p>

<h2 id="h-how-the-bear-paw-grip-affects-accuracy">How Your Bear Bow Grip Affects Accuracy</h2>
<p>Most accuracy problems people blame on a bow start in the bow hand. A good grip doesn&rsquo;t shoot for you; it makes good form easier to repeat.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Same hand position every shot</strong> means the bow reacts the same way every shot.</li>
  <li><strong>Relaxed fingers</strong> let the bow jump forward cleanly instead of twisting.</li>
  <li><strong>Pressure on the thumb pad, not the palm,</strong> lines the force up with your forearm bones.</li>
</ul>
<p>Draw length plays a part too. If your draw is too long, your bow arm locks and the grip pressure shifts. If you shoot a compound, check your fit with our <?= post_link('2016-pse-draw-length-adjustment', 'draw length adjustment guide') ?> before you blame the grip.</p>
