<h2 id="h-torque-reduction-and-bow-reaction">Torque Reduction and Bow Reaction</h2>
<p>Torque is any twisting force your hand puts into the riser. It shows up as arrows that hit left or right of your aim even though your pin or point looked perfect.</p>
<h3 id="h-signs-your-grip-is-causing-torque">Signs Your Grip Is Causing Torque</h3>
<ul class="checks">
  <li>Left-right misses while elevation stays consistent</li>
  <li>The bow rotates or cants after the shot</li>
  <li>Your knuckles are white or fingers curl around the grip at full draw</li>
  <li>Groups open up when you are tired or cold</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="h-how-to-reduce-torque">How to Reduce Torque</h3>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Set your hand before you draw.</strong> Place the grip on the pad of your thumb, knuckles at roughly 45 degrees.</li>
  <li><strong>Let your fingers hang loose.</strong> Use a wrist or finger sling so you don&rsquo;t worry about dropping the bow.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep pressure centered.</strong> Avoid pushing with the heel on one side of the grip.</li>
  <li><strong>Consider a slimmer grip.</strong> Less contact area means less room for twisting.</li>
</ol>
<?= callout('tip', 'The sling trick', 'A simple ' . amz('compound bow wrist sling', 'wrist sling') . ' often fixes grabbing faster than any new grip. Once you trust the bow won&rsquo;t fall, your hand relaxes on its own.') ?>

<h2 id="h-comfort-during-long-shooting-sessions">Comfort During Long Shooting Sessions</h2>
<p>A grip that feels fine for ten arrows can cause pressure points after a hundred. Watch for soreness at the base of your thumb or along the lifeline of your palm: that usually means the grip is pushing your hand into one spot too hard.</p>
<p>Simple fixes include a thin <?= amz('bow grip tape', 'bow grip tape') ?> to change texture, a <?= amz('traditional bow grip wrap leather', 'leather grip wrap') ?> on a traditional bow for warmth and padding, or a thinner side plate on a compound. Change one thing at a time and shoot a full session before judging.</p>
