<h3 id="h-bear-archery-vs-bearpaw">Bear Archery vs Bearpaw: Not the Same Company</h3>
<p>If you searched &ldquo;bearpaw grips&rdquo; or &ldquo;bear claw bow grips,&rdquo; you may be looking for something else entirely. <strong>Bearpaw</strong> (Bearpaw Bows / Bearpaw Products) is a German traditional archery brand that makes bows and accessories. It is a separate company from <strong>Bear Archery</strong>, the American bow maker founded on Fred Bear&rsquo;s legacy. If you want Bearpaw-branded grip accessories, shop for that brand specifically and check compatibility with your bow.</p>

<h2 id="h-how-to-test-grip-fit">How to Test Whether a Bow Grip Fits Your Hand</h2>
<p>At Better Outdoors we recommend a simple test before you buy a bow or replace a grip:</p>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Hold without drawing.</strong> Your hand should close naturally with the grip on your thumb pad, not jammed into your palm.</li>
  <li><strong>Draw and hold.</strong> At full draw, your fingers should be able to hang loose without the bow rotating.</li>
  <li><strong>Shoot a dozen arrows.</strong> Watch for left-right misses and how the bow reacts after the shot.</li>
  <li><strong>Check for pressure points</strong> after 30 or more arrows.</li>
  <li><strong>Try a contrast.</strong> Shoot a slimmer or fuller grip back to back; your groups usually pick the winner.</li>
</ol>
